{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UQY2Sgo7FYaQVxnE57e5Gt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: dependency-map\n2\ttitle: \"Shared Component Dependency Map\"\n3\t\n4\toverview: |\n5\t  We map each of the 14 utilities to the shared pkg/ components they will need in\n6\t  their Go implementations. Dependencies are derived from C source analysis in\n7\t  sources.yaml. The map justifies the scope and required API of each shared component\n8\t  SRD (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format). We list a utility under a component when\n9\t  it requires behavior that would be duplicated across cmd/ packages without that component.\n10\t\n11\tcomponents:\n12\t  - id: pkg/testutils\n13\t    rationale: |\n14\t      Every utility is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew GNU reference\n15\t      binary side by side and comparing stdout, stderr, and exit code. This logic is\n16\t      identical for all utilities: execute both binaries with the same stdin, args, and\n17\t      environment; diff the outputs; report divergence. Without a shared harness each\n18\t      cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison logic. Additionally,\n19\t      some utilities require output normalization before comparison (timestamps in ts,\n20\t      path prefixes in find), and a shared hook mechanism avoids duplicating that too.\n21\t    users:\n22\t      - utility: ts\n23\t        package: moreutils\n24\t        need: timestamp normalization hook (output contains wall-clock timestamps that differ between runs)\n25\t      - utility: sponge\n26\t        package: moreutils\n27\t        need: file-state comparison after execution (output is a written file, not stdout)\n28\t      - utility: vidir\n29\t        package: moreutils\n30\t        need: file-state comparison (renames files; no stdout output to diff)\n31\t      - utility: pee\n32\t        package: moreutils\n33\t        need: stdout comparison; multiple subcommands mean ordering may need normalization\n34\t      - utility: chronic\n35\t        package: moreutils\n36\t        need: stdout/stderr suppression behavior; exit-code passthrough verification\n37\t      - utility: ls\n38\t        package: coreutils\n39\t        need: timestamp normalization (-l shows mtime); path normalization for test fixtures\n40\t      - utility: cat\n41\t        package: coreutils\n42\t        need: standard stdout/stderr diff; binary-input test cases\n43\t      - utility: cp\n44\t        package: coreutils\n45\t        need: file-state comparison (output is a copied file); no stdout to diff\n46\t      - utility: mv\n47\t        package: coreutils\n48\t        need: file-state comparison (output is a moved file); no stdout to diff\n49\t      - utility: wc\n50\t        package: coreutils\n51\t        need: standard stdout diff; all output is deterministic counts\n52\t      - utility: du\n53\t        package: coreutils\n54\t        need: path and size normalization (sizes vary by filesystem block size across machines)\n55\t      - utility: grep\n56\t        package: grep\n57\t        need: standard stdout/stderr diff; exit-code verification (0/1/2 contract)\n58\t      - utility: find\n59\t        package: findutils\n60\t        need: path normalization (output order varies by filesystem); file-state comparison for -exec\n61\t      - utility: xargs\n62\t        package: findutils\n63\t        need: stdout comparison; exec order normalization for -P (parallel)\n64\t\n65\t  - id: pkg/sys\n66\t    rationale: |\n67\t      Several utilities need syscall surfaces that diverge between Darwin and Linux:\n68\t      terminal width queries (ioctl TIOCGWINSZ), file metadata beyond what os.Stat\n69\t      provides (device number, inode, hard-link count for ls -l), and signal handling\n70\t      (SIGPIPE for clean exit when stdout closes, SIGWINCH for ls to re-query terminal\n71\t      width on resize). Go's standard library does not provide a stable cross-platform\n72\t      API for these. Wrapping them in pkg/sys avoids per-utility cgo or syscall imports\n73\t      and ensures the Darwin/Linux divergence is handled once.\n74\t    users:\n75\t      - utility: ls\n76\t        package: coreutils\n77\t        needs:\n78\t          - terminal_width: |\n79\t              ls.c:2281 uses struct winsize via ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) to determine column\n80\t              count for multi-column layout. Go's standard library has no portable\n81\t              terminal-width query; pkg/sys wraps golang.org/x/term or syscall.\n82\t          - file_metadata: |\n83\t              ls -l needs st_nlink (hard-link count), st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev (device\n84\t              numbers for character/block devices), and st_blocks. os.FileInfo does not\n85\t              expose these; pkg/sys exposes them via syscall.Stat_t.\n86\t          - signal_handling: |\n87\t              ls.c:1600-1620 sets up SIGPIPE (suppress broken-pipe on stdout close) and\n88\t              ls.c:2281 registers SIGWINCH to re-query terminal width on resize. Go's\n89\t              os/signal package provides the hook; pkg/sys provides a consistent interface.\n90\t          - lstat: |\n91\t              ls.c uses lstat for symlink targets (to show link target vs target info\n92\t              depending on -L flag). os.Lstat is available but symlink metadata\n93\t              (st_mode of the link itself) requires care on Darwin.\n94\t\n95\t      - utility: du\n96\t        package: coreutils\n97\t        needs:\n98\t          - file_metadata: |\n99\t              du.c:554 uses fts_statp (stat struct from fts traversal) to get st_blocks\n100\t              and st_dev for deduplication of hard-linked files. pkg/sys exposes st_dev\n101\t              and st_ino for hard-link detection and st_blocks for physical block count.\n102\t          - signal_handling: |\n103\t              du.c uses SIGPIPE for clean stdout close.\n104\t\n105\t      - utility: find\n106\t        package: findutils\n107\t        needs:\n108\t          - file_metadata: |\n109\t              find/ftsfind.c:172 calls apply_predicate with a stat struct for -type, -size,\n110\t              -newer, -perm predicates. The full stat struct (st_mode, st_size, st_mtime,\n111\t              st_uid, st_gid, st_nlink) is needed. pkg/sys exposes this.\n112\t          - signal_handling: |\n113\t              find/ftsfind.c:735-749 registers SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 for debug output. pkg/sys\n114\t              provides a clean signal registration interface.\n115\t          - lstat: |\n116\t              find uses lstat (not stat) by default to avoid following symlinks unless\n117\t              -L is given. pkg/sys wraps os.Lstat with full stat metadata.\n118\t\n119\t      - utility: xargs\n120\t        package: findutils\n121\t        needs:\n122\t          - signal_handling: |\n123\t              xargs propagates signals to child processes (SIGTERM, SIGINT). pkg/sys\n124\t              provides signal forwarding to process groups.\n125\t\n126\t  - id: pkg/format\n127\t    rationale: |\n128\t      Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n129\t      fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n130\t      file types, and both ls and du convert byte counts to human-readable sizes\n131\t      (K/M/G/T). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own column\n132\t      math and ANSI escape handling, producing inconsistent output and making\n133\t      differential testing harder.\n134\t    users:\n135\t      - utility: ls\n136\t        package: coreutils\n137\t        needs:\n138\t          - column_alignment: |\n139\t              ls.c uses multi-column layout (default) and long format (-l). Column widths\n140\t              are computed from the longest filename or field value in the listing. pkg/format\n141\t              provides a column-width computation function and a right/left-pad formatter.\n142\t          - ansi_color: |\n143\t              ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h. ls outputs ANSI escape sequences for file\n144\t              types (directory=blue, symlink=cyan, executable=green, etc.) and resets\n145\t              color after each entry. pkg/format provides a file-type-to-ANSI-code\n146\t              lookup and a color-strip mode when stdout is not a TTY.\n147\t          - human_readable_size: |\n148\t              ls -h and ls -s --block-size=human both need human_readable() output.\n149\t              pkg/format provides byte-to-unit conversion with configurable suffixes\n150\t              (K/M/G/T) and SI vs binary rounding modes.\n151\t\n152\t      - utility: du\n153\t        package: coreutils\n154\t        needs:\n155\t          - human_readable_size: |\n156\t              du.c:153 declares human_output_opts; du.c:442 calls human_readable().\n157\t              du -h outputs sizes like 4.0K, 128M. The same conversion logic as ls -h.\n158\t              pkg/format provides this as a shared function to avoid duplication.\n159\t\n160\tutilities_without_shared_components:\n161\t  - utility: cat\n162\t    package: coreutils\n163\t    reason: |\n164\t      cat is pure I/O passthrough with flag-controlled output transformations (-n, -b,\n165\t      -s, -v, -E, -T). It needs no terminal-width query, no file-metadata beyond\n166\t      fstat for identity check (Go's os.SameFile covers this), no formatting, and\n167\t      no ANSI color. cat does not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n168\t\n169\t  - utility: wc\n170\t    package: coreutils\n171\t    reason: |\n172\t      wc counts lines, words, and bytes. It uses fstat only to detect when input and\n173\t      output are the same file (no practical relevance in Go). Column alignment for\n174\t      multi-file output is simple right-padding of numeric fields, not tabular\n175\t      formatting. wc does not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n176\t\n177\t  - utility: ts\n178\t    package: moreutils\n179\t    reason: |\n180\t      ts reads stdin line by line and prepends timestamps using POSIX strftime and\n181\t      gettimeofday/clock_gettime (exposed via Go's time.Now(), time.Since(), and\n182\t      time.Format()). Monotonic clock is available via time.Now() in Go. ts does\n183\t      not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n184\t\n185\t  - utility: sponge\n186\t    package: moreutils\n187\t    reason: |\n188\t      sponge reads stdin into a buffer, then writes to a file using a temp-file and\n189\t      rename for atomicity. All operations are standard file I/O (os.CreateTemp,\n190\t      os.Rename). sponge does not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n191\t\n192\t  - utility: pee\n193\t    package: moreutils\n194\t    reason: |\n195\t      pee forks a subprocess for each argv command and tees stdin to all of them.\n196\t      Go's os/exec and io.MultiWriter cover this without any pkg/ wrapping. pee\n197\t      does not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n198\t\n199\t  - utility: vidir\n200\t    package: moreutils\n201\t    reason: |\n202\t      vidir opens $EDITOR with a temp file listing directory entries. All operations\n203\t      are standard file I/O and os/exec. vidir does not use pkg/sys or pkg/format.\n204\t\n205\t  - utility: chronic\n206\t    package: moreutils\n207\t    reason: |\n208\t      chronic runs a subcommand and suppresses its output unless it fails. All\n209\t      operations are os/exec and io.ReadAll. chronic does not use pkg/sys or\n210\t      pkg/format.\n211\t\n212\t  - utility: cp\n213\t    package: coreutils\n214\t    reason: |\n215\t      cp is handled in a later phase (coreutils phase 3). Not in the current batch.\n216\t\n217\t  - utility: mv\n218\t    package: coreutils\n219\t    reason: |\n220\t      mv is handled in a later phase (coreutils phase 3). Not in the current batch.\n221\t\n222\t  - utility: grep\n223\t    package: grep\n224\t    reason: |\n225\t      grep is handled in a later phase (search utilities phase 4). Not in the current batch.\n226\t\n227\t  - utility: find\n228\t    package: findutils\n229\t    reason: |\n230\t      find is handled in a later phase (search utilities phase 4). pkg/sys dependency\n231\t      is noted above but the SRD and use case are deferred.\n232\t\n233\t  - utility: xargs\n234\t    package: findutils\n235\t    reason: |\n236\t      xargs is handled in a later phase (search utilities phase 4). Not in the current batch.\n237\t\n238\tnotes:\n239\t  - |\n240\t    egrep and fgrep are shell script wrappers for grep -E and grep -F respectively.\n241\t    They do not require separate Go implementations; they are handled as flags of the\n242\t    grep SRD.\n243\t  - |\n244\t    The current batch (phase 1-2) focuses on ts, wc, cat, sponge from the dependency\n245\t    analysis. These four utilities share only pkg/testutils and require no pkg/sys or\n246\t    pkg/format, making them the lowest-risk starting point for the pipeline.\n247\t  - |\n248\t    ls requires all three shared components (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format) and\n249\t    is the most complex utility in the batch. It is deferred to phase 3 (coreutils).\n250\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fv7SiFKPRAfJ3p2EskazmV","session_id":"35afd508-ec89-42dc-b408-e4f7fabc1198","uuid":"24a3c258-2801-4229-b8e6-fc4add9c88fa","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:17:21.585Z"}
