{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01U26bfUEEa58bGqhZsFWWDe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so R3.1, R3.2, R3.3, R3.4 are all marked as `in_progress` for srd081-basenc. These are the ones I need to implement/verify for this task.\n\nLet me re-read what each requirement is:\n- R3.1: `-i` or `--ignore-garbage` must ignore non-alphabet characters during decoding.\n- R3.2: Must read from FILE or stdin (when no file or \"-\" is given).\n- R3.3: Must exit 1 when no encoding scheme is specified, when the file cannot be opened, or when decode input is invalid.\n\n\n- R3.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nLooking at the existing code, all 4 are already implemented. But I should verify the implementation is correct and the tests actually pass against gbasenc. I'm also noticing a potential issue where parseArgs silently uses the last positional argument when multiple are provided, but gbasenc would error with \"extra operand\" instead. This could be causing test failures.\n\nActually, checking the existing tests, they don't use multiple file arguments, so this wouldn't trigger a failure. Still, for correctness I should add that validation. Now I'm examining the stripGarbage function to see if there are any issues with how it handles the ignore-garbage flag that might cause differential test failures.\n\nThe stripGarbage function removes characters outside of `A-Za-z0-9+/=-_`, but for base16 decoding, only `0-9A-Fa-f` are valid. When using `-i` with `--base16`, gbasenc would strip everything that's not hex, but the current implementation might let invalid characters like `g` or `+` through to the decoder, causing it to fail. Let me test what gbasenc actually does with base16 and garbage input... I'm realizing that `stripGarbage` is too permissive for base16 since it keeps uppercase letters beyond 'F', which aren't valid hex characters, though this is